  {
   "title": "The troubleshooting procedure is printed as two sections whose numbering does not join up",
   "text": "§5-19 has nine steps and §5-20 (Magnitude Control Circuit) has five, printed as 1 to 5. But §5-19 step 6 tells you, when the voltage across C1 is much greater than 227 V, to go to the magnitude control troubleshooting 'starting at step 10' -- a step 10 that does not exist in either section as printed. Nine plus five is fourteen, and 10 is the next number after §5-19's last, so §5-20's steps were 10 to 14 before a reprint renumbered them and left the cross-reference behind. The procedure here is the combined 1 to 14 sequence, which makes that reference land on the square wave generator check where it was written to land. Every step still cites the section it is printed in, so it can be found in the manual.",
   "refs": [
    "C1"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "C1's voltage rating differs by a factor of ten between the parts list and the schematic",
   "text": "Table 6-18 gives C1 as CAP,POLYES,0.72UF,+-20%,1300V. Figure 7-17 sheet 1 annotates the same part 0.72 uF, 13 kV. The capacitance agrees; only the rating disagrees, and by 10x. This is the main HVDC filter, sitting across a rail that reaches about 1100 V in normal use, so the difference matters for a replacement. Read the marking on the fitted part before sourcing one.",
   "refs": [
    "C1"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "§2-167 names a switch U5C that does not exist on this board",
   "text": "The theory of operation says 'Switch U5C is connected to the AMPLITUDE voltage.' There is no U5 in Table 6-18 and none on the schematic -- the parts list runs U1, U2, U3, U4, U6, U7 and up. The schematic and the rest of the same paragraph both show U7C, which is one of the two DG444 switches that chop between +AMPLITUDE and -AMPLITUDE. Read it as U7C.",
   "refs": [
    "U7",
    "U3"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Fault codes 3131 to 3133 are titled 100 Hz and specify 50 Hz",
   "text": "All three are titled 'HV AC 100Hz, Output', and all three bodies say the instrument is configured with an amplitude at 50 Hz. Nothing else distinguishes them from 3128 to 3130, which are the same three amplitudes at 1 kHz. Either way the group tests operation below 120 Hz, where K14 to K16 series the transformer windings, so what it tells you is the same; but if you are reproducing the condition by hand, the body is the specific instruction and the title is not.",
   "refs": [
    "K14",
    "K15",
    "K16"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Five parts have different Fluke stock numbers in the two revisions",
   "text": "R1 and R2 (644259 in 1996, 159608 in Rev 9), R3 to R5 (686774 / 109926), R22 (641115 / 109090), R70 and R71 (543355 / 220046) and VR1 (260695 / 820431). The descriptions are identical in both lists, and four of the five are carbon composition resistors, which reads as a supplier change rather than a design change. The Rev 9 numbers are recorded as 'as built' since Rev 9 is the revision the drawing and the photograph match.",
   "refs": [
    "R1",
    "R2",
    "R3",
    "R4",
    "R5",
    "R22",
    "R70",
    "R71",
    "VR1"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Table 6-18 prints K13 as K013, and the Rev 9 list prints J1 as Z1",
   "text": "In the 1996 text layer the relay group reads 'K3,K5,K6 K9, K12,K013 K15, K16'. The leading zero is a typo for K13; the row's quantity of 8 confirms it. Separately, the scanned Rev 9 list renders the J1 connector row's designator as 'z1', which is a real and different part on this board -- Z1 is the 10 kilohm SIP resistor network, stock 412924. Both are corrected in the build, the second by stock number since no designator-keyed correction could tell the two Z1s apart.",
   "refs": [
    "K13",
    "J1",
    "Z1"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "The photograph was taken with the high voltage shield fitted",
   "text": "MP3 covers roughly the right-hand 40% of the board, so transformer T1, capacitor C1, connector P611 and relays K3, K4, K12 and K13 are not visible in it. Those parts have positions on the drawing and on the schematic sheets but none on the photograph, and the photo alignment is solved from landmarks in the visible portion only, so treat it as approximate. Drawing, Overlay and Swipe are reliable on the left-hand side of the board and tell you nothing on the right.",
   "refs": [
    "MP3",
    "T1",
    "C1",
    "P611"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Sheet 4 is a table, not a schematic",
   "text": "Figure 7-17 sheet 4 carries no circuit. It is the relay state table for both assemblies: for each of fourteen instrument configurations -- dormant, ac and dc 220 V, ac and dc 1100 V, ac and dc 2.2 A, and the calibration setups -- it gives the position of every relay and FET on A14 and A15. R is the reset position of a latching relay, S the set position, and a mark means a non-latching relay is energised or a FET is on. It is the fastest way to answer 'should this relay be pulled in right now', so it is extracted and readable, but no component positions are placed on it.",
   "refs": [
    "K7"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "How far each set of positions was checked",
   "text": "Board: every one of the 177 parts was accounted for by eye — 161 markers reviewed tier by tier and 16 recorded as having no silkscreen designator. The reader placed 140 of them and 123 of those were right; 74 are now placed or corrected by hand, of which 30 were redrawn afterwards as the component's own body rather than its designator — the large parts especially, where the marker now covers T1's whole footprint, C1's can and U9's 40-pin package instead of the lettering beside them. Schematic sheet 1: every placed marker reviewed the same way, and a second reader (a vision-language model) run over the whole sheet as a cross-check, which independently flagged 12 of the 28 markers the review had already rejected. Sheet 2: the ambiguous and single tiers reviewed in full, the agreed tier sampled. Sheet 3: the agreed tier reviewed in full and the rest dropped except six spot-checked by hand. So a board marker is as good as this pipeline gets; a sheet-2 or sheet-3 marker in the agreed tier is probable rather than confirmed. Every marker carries the tier it came from, and the app shows it.",
   "refs": []
  },
  {
   "title": "Manufacturer part numbers come from OCR of a scan and carry character errors",
   "text": "The 1996 parts list has a real text layer and its designators, descriptions and Fluke stock numbers are exact. The manufacturer supply code and manufacturer part number exist only in the scanned Rev 9 list, and at that type size tesseract confuses 0 with O, 5 with S, and picks up the column rule as a leading letter -- VR1's 1N754A reads as 'J1N754ARR1', R35's MF50DVT1963F as 'IMFSODVT1963F'. The Fluke stock number is the identifier to order against; treat the manufacturer part number as a hint to be checked, not as data.",
   "refs": [
    "VR1",
    "R35"
   ]
  }
